    "body": "> My first question here would be, why `translatable-data-*` and not `data-translatable-*`? With the former there'd be an expectation for a JS accessor like `.translatableDataset` to be introduced, to match the existing [`.dataset`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HTMLElement/dataset). With the latter, JS code could continue to use `.dataset` and the only change you'd actually be asking for the prefix to be listed as a translatable attribute.\n\nFair point. The reason I started with `translatable-data-*` is that is shouldn't be a compat problem. With `data-translatable-*`, that cuts into the existing [namespace](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#custom-data-attribute), so there might already be folks using those. (Though given the name, they'd likely expect them to be translatable...) But sure! I do like the idea of making them work with `.dataset`.\n\n> Second, I don't think it's very common to attach `data-*` attributes on custom elements, so the usage there seems like a bit of a stretch. Rather, it seems like you're pointing at an inability to easily signal the translatability of attributes on custom elements, and that while that problem would be ameliorated by making `data-translatable-*` attributes translatable, it's not necessarily the only or best solution for them.\n\nI'm not sure what you're saying here. Are you saying custom elements just don't often use attributes? (I don't find that to be true. Perhaps they use IDL properties, but those should reflect.) Perhaps you're saying it's more common to use non-`data-`-prefixed attributes? If so, maybe, but that violates the spec so I don't want to encourage it further.",
